  [1mStorage.[0m One versioned object in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m under [38;5;153mtasksapp:v1[39m, holding every collection below plus a [38;5;153mschemaVersion[39m.
  A single write path keeps the log and task state consistent, and a [38;5;153mBroadcastChannel[39m publishes each committed change so
  other tabs reload and re-derive. That channel is the seam a server's push stream would replace.

  [1mCollections.[0m
  users         [{ id, name }]                        // local roster, no auth
  currentUserId                                        // the identity picker
  tasks         [{ id, title, description, status,
                   assigneeId, creatorId, dueDate, deletedAt }]
  events        [{ id, taskId, actorId, type, field,
                   from, to, timestamp }]              // append-only, the truth
  subscriptions [{ userId, taskId, mode }]             // mode: 'watch' | 'mute'
  readState     { [userId]: { readEventIds: [...] } }
  [38;5;153msubscriptions[39m stores [3monly[0m explicit overrides — implicit creator/assignee subscription is computed, never written. That
  keeps "I'm subscribed because I'm the assignee" from going stale when the assignee changes.

  [1mModules[0m — each one testable alone, with the domain layer entirely pure:
  - [38;5;153mstore/store.js[39m — load/persist, [38;5;153mcommit(mutation)[39m, cross-tab broadcast. The only impure module.
  - [38;5;153mdomain/diff.js[39m — [38;5;153m(taskBefore, taskAfter, actorId) → events[][39m. Owns which fields are notifiable.
  - [38;5;153mdomain/subscriptions.js[39m — [38;5;153m(userId, task, subscriptions) → boolean[39m. Owns mute-beats-implicit.
  - [38;5;153mdomain/notifications.js[39m — [38;5;153m(events, tasks, subscriptions, userId, readState) → { items, unreadCount }[39m. Owns
    self-suppression and collapsing.
  - [38;5;153mui/[39m — task list, identity picker, bell + inbox panel, toast host. Views read derived state; they never compute it.

  The whole feature's correctness lives in three pure functions. The UI and the store stay boring, which is deliberate —
  it's what makes the unit tests meaningful rather than DOM-shaped.

  [1mOne honest wrinkle:[0m collapsing means a notification can cover several events, so read state keys on the group's newest
  event id. Marking a group read then leaves older events in it unread-but-hidden; re-deriving after a new event on
  that task produces a fresh group that is correctly unread. I've traced it and it behaves, but it's the subtlest part
  of the design and I'd call it out for review.

  Does this section look right before I move on to behavior rules and UI?

